# Host-platform build artifacts — must NOT leak into the Linux build context.
# Without this, `COPY . .` in apps/api/Dockerfile copies the host's own
# node_modules (e.g. macOS @esbuild/darwin-arm64) into the image, and
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` does not always replace already-present
# native optional-dependency binaries — causing esbuild/tsx to fail at
# runtime with "you installed esbuild for another platform" inside the
# Linux container. See docs/deployment-runbook.md staging validation notes.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# apps/* and root `dist/` are excluded — those get built fresh in-container.
# packages/*/dist is intentionally NOT excluded (see apps/api/Dockerfile's
# comment on the build step): this Docker Desktop environment was found to
# unreliably emit .js/.d.ts output for packages/schemas, packages/model-router
# and packages/prompt-engine when their tsc build runs against COPY'd (not
# bind-mounted) source — reproduced consistently across BuildKit and the
# legacy builder, but never on the host directly. Until that environment
# issue is root-caused, the pragmatic workaround is to ship each package's
# already-host-built dist/ in the image and only build @grafista/api fresh
# in-container. This means `pnpm run build` must have been run on the HOST
# for packages/schemas, packages/model-router and packages/prompt-engine
# before `docker compose -f docker-compose.staging.yml build` — see
# docs/staging-compose.md and docs/deployment-runbook.md.
/dist/
apps/*/dist/
.next/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

.git/
.env
.env.local
.env.production
.env.staging

uploads/
generated/
*.psd

*.log
logs/
coverage/
.nyc_output/
.test-pgdata/
